<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="QUM98wUlg3vjXlxixsFmr1" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="N5T8Hmh2rBlRvdNukHTcWt" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="LfiIFAo7gIkZMGh4IwEuAa" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="DoLRYH8yLewYZ15hO6zgIV" Location="VL.Mapsui" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="PxhKg8BscZY3FZ5BBq5V6S">
    <Canvas Id="GO0StuvySIilC684AxZCtY" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="C7TpQCTkK0oo15gfZD0c0o">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="KxpWAxhkSyww0ws29p3F9Z">
        <Canvas Id="HV5mZB2AHtDeSwYQHkHv8E" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="EskmCzlInzRJezMaD9KkUP" Bounds="-1555,-119,905,1949" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="06 - Change how the world looks.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;DRAG IT. SCROLL IT. Then switch Enabled on, because it starts off: opening a document in vvvv RUNS&#xD;&#xA;it, and a map that fetched the moment you opened it would give you no chance to decline.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ONE KNOB, AND IT IS AN INTEGER. Everything this map is made of - every road, every coastline, every&#xD;&#xA;colour - arrives as small square pictures from a server. WHICH server is a preset, and Basemap picks&#xD;&#xA;it. Turn it and the whole world changes appearance. Nothing else has to move.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  0  OpenTopoMap     contours and hillshade&#xD;&#xA;  1  OpenStreetMap   the one everybody knows&#xD;&#xA;  2  CyclOSM         built for street level - zoom IN before judging this one&#xD;&#xA;  3  Satellite       Sentinel-2, a whole year with the clouds taken out&#xD;&#xA;  4  Night lights    NASA Black Marble. Cities are the only thing left&#xD;&#xA;  5  Terrain Light   almost nothing: land, water, relief&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Out-of-range is safe: Switch is built on GetSlice, which wraps. Type 9 and you get preset 3.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE CREDIT TRAVELS WITH THE TILES, and that is the reason for the second Switch. Almost every tile&#xD;&#xA;service requires credit ON THE MAP, and a layer carrying none contributes nothing to the Attribution&#xD;&#xA;widget. Two Switches on one Index means the credit cannot be left behind on the wrong basemap -&#xD;&#xA;which is easy to do, entirely invisible, and turns complying into appearing to.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;If you add a seventh preset, ADD IT TO BOTH. A missing pin gives an empty string in silence. When&#xD;&#xA;the list is long enough for that to be a real risk, the answer is a record with a Url field and an&#xD;&#xA;Attribution field so the two cannot be separated - VL.Mapsui's HowTo Draw many features builds one.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;TWO BASEMAPS THAT WORK PERFECTLY AND ARE NOT ALLOWED, which is the more useful lesson: CARTO's&#xD;&#xA;(their own licence restricts the tile service to enterprise customers) and Esri's World Imagery&#xD;&#xA;(granted only for tracing features). Both return a correct, good-looking tile if you ask. A REQUEST&#xD;&#xA;THAT SUCCEEDS TELLS YOU NOTHING ABOUT WHETHER YOU WERE ALLOWED TO MAKE IT.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Presets 3, 4 and 5 come from EOX's demo service, which rate-limits and offers no guarantee. Being a&#xD;&#xA;good guest is why the cache matters.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE MOUSE IS WIRED, NOT BUILT IN. Nothing in VL.Mapsui decides what a drag means; the links below&#xD;&#xA;are a choice, and swapping the mouse for an LFO or an OSC message means changing them and nothing&#xD;&#xA;else. Two things there are worth knowing before you copy the pattern:&#xD;&#xA;  - Console is a LAYER. Notifications travel through the Skia layer graph, so anything that wants&#xD;&#xA;    the mouse has to sit in that graph - which is why its Output goes into the Group.&#xD;&#xA;  - Wheel State ACCUMULATES, so it goes through FrameDifference. Measured in vvvv 7.4: one notch&#xD;&#xA;    moves the difference by exactly 1, which is why Notch Size is 1. Windows says 120 per notch, and&#xD;&#xA;    that mismatch once meant a wheel that turned and a map that did not move, with nothing on screen&#xD;&#xA;    to say why.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT TO WATCH:&#xD;&#xA;  Layers Built  goes up by ONE each time you change the preset, then stops. Panning and zooming must&#xD;&#xA;                NOT move it - where you look never rebuilds the layer, only what the source IS does.&#xD;&#xA;                If it climbs on its own, CLOSE VVVV: every rebuild starts a fresh round of tile&#xD;&#xA;                requests, and that once exhausted a machine of ephemeral ports and took a home&#xD;&#xA;                network down.&#xD;&#xA;  Cache Status  where tiles are kept, and how much. Each basemap gets its OWN folder in there, so&#xD;&#xA;                GOING BACK TO A PRESET YOU HAVE ALREADY SEEN COSTS NOTHING - it comes off the disk&#xD;&#xA;                and never touches the network. Try it: pick 3, then 0, then 3 again.&#xD;&#xA;                It did not work that way until 2026-08-17, and the bug was found by this patch:&#xD;&#xA;                with one shared folder the second basemap you picked never fetched anything,&#xD;&#xA;                because the first one's tiles already sat under the same {zoom}/{x}/{y} name.&#xD;&#xA;                Changing the preset appeared to do nothing, and every status pin said it worked.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;MEASURED, so you do not have to take &quot;the cache helps&quot; on trust. Turn Diagnostics on and the&#xD;&#xA;overlay's last line times each switch from the moment the layers change until the picture stops&#xD;&#xA;loading. On this machine, 22 tiles at a fixed viewport:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  cold (never seen before)   2216 - 2547 ms, across three different servers&#xD;&#xA;  warm (already on disk)     never registered as busy on a single frame&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That second row is a floor, not a zero: it means the fetch finished faster than one frame could&#xD;&#xA;sample it, so it is under about 17 ms. At least two orders of magnitude, and the disk agrees -&#xD;&#xA;nothing at all was written during the warm switches.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Note the &quot;#N&quot; counter beside the figure. It is there because the FIRST version of this measurement&#xD;&#xA;had no counter, printed the same 2249 ms for three different basemaps, and looked entirely&#xD;&#xA;convincing: a switch that produced no reading left the previous switch's number on screen. An&#xD;&#xA;instrument has to make its own staleness visible, or a reader cannot tell one from the other.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS PATCH USES ONE PACKAGE, VL.Mapsui, which is normally a reason to live in that package's own&#xD;&#xA;help folder rather than here. It is the exemption written into this pack's rules: a spine Tutorial&#xD;&#xA;may need one package when the sequence requires it - and the map act opens by adding exactly one&#xD;&#xA;capability, a basemap, nothing else.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHERE TO GO NEXT - three different directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 07                 put your own shapes on it&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt Paint by numbers     how little can a map be made of? No tiles at all, for a start&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt Live earthquakes     the first live data - dots sized by the ground moving">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
